### Why does conflict frequency matter?

The choice between optimistic and pessimistic locking is fundamentally a contention question. When conflict probability is low, optimistic locking wins because no locks are held and the rare conflict costs just one cheap retry. When conflict probability is high, pessimistic locking wins because it serializes access without wasting work on failed retries.

```java
// Low contention example:
// Rajesh buys a rarely traded government bond.
// Almost no one else touches that row concurrently.
// Optimistic locking: no lock overhead, rare retry.
// Pessimistic locking: unnecessary lock, wasted concurrency.

// High contention example:
// 5,000 investors buy a hot IPO stock simultaneously.
// One row (stock quantity) is hit by 5,000 transactions.
// Optimistic locking: 4,999 retries, many retry again -> retry storm.
// Pessimistic locking: investors queue, each succeeds in turn.
```

### What happens under high contention with optimistic locking?

With high contention, optimistic locking produces a cascade of failures. Many transactions read the same version, all try to decrement the stock quantity, one succeeds, and the rest fail. On retry, they conflict again because the rate of incoming requests exceeds the rate of successful commits.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.StockHolding;
import com.bank.repository.StockRepository;
import org.springframework.orm.ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OptimisticStockService {

    private final StockRepository stockRepo;

    public OptimisticStockService(StockRepository stockRepo) {
        this.stockRepo = stockRepo;
    }

    // Under high contention, this causes a retry storm
    public boolean buyStock(String symbol, int quantity) {
        int retries = 5;
        while (retries > 0) {
            try {
                return doAttemptBuy(symbol, quantity);
            } catch (ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException e) {
                retries--;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    @Transactional
    public boolean doAttemptBuy(String symbol, int qty) {
        StockHolding stock = stockRepo.findBySymbol(symbol);
        if (stock.getAvailableQuantity() < qty) return false;
        stock.setAvailableQuantity(
            stock.getAvailableQuantity() - qty);
        stockRepo.save(stock);
        return true;
    }
}
```

```text
5,000 concurrent buy requests for symbol "ICICIIPL":
  Round 1: 5,000 read version=1. 1 succeeds. 4,999 fail.
  Round 2: 4,999 retry. Read version=2. 1 succeeds. 4,998 fail.
  Round 3: 4,998 retry. 1 succeeds. 4,997 fail.
  Total wasted transactions: ~25,000 failed attempts.
  This is a retry storm.
```

### How does pessimistic locking handle hot rows?

Pessimistic locking serializes access to the hot row. Each transaction acquires an exclusive lock, decrements the quantity, and commits. No work is wasted because each transaction succeeds in its turn.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.StockHolding;
import com.bank.repository.StockRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class PessimisticStockService {

    private final StockRepository stockRepo;

    public PessimisticStockService(StockRepository stockRepo) {
        this.stockRepo = stockRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public boolean buyStock(String symbol, int quantity) {
        StockHolding stock = stockRepo.findForUpdateBySymbol(symbol);
        // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE. Row locked. Others wait.

        if (stock.getAvailableQuantity() < quantity) {
            return false;
        }
        stock.setAvailableQuantity(
            stock.getAvailableQuantity() - quantity);
        stockRepo.save(stock);
        return true;
    }
}
```

```text
5,000 concurrent buy requests with pessimistic locking:
  Thread 1: lock, decrement, commit. Success.
  Thread 2: lock (after T1), decrement, commit. Success.
  ...
  Thread 10: lock. Quantity=0. Return false.
  No wasted work. Each thread succeeds or reports sold-out.
```

### What is an atomic conditional UPDATE?

An atomic conditional UPDATE pushes the check-and-modify logic into a single SQL statement, eliminating the read-modify-write race entirely without holding an application-level lock.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.StockHolding;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Modifying;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;

public interface StockRepository extends JpaRepository<StockHolding, Long> {

    @Modifying
    @Query("UPDATE StockHolding s "
         + "SET s.availableQuantity = s.availableQuantity - :qty "
         + "WHERE s.symbol = :symbol "
         + "AND s.availableQuantity >= :qty")
    int decrementStock(String symbol, int qty);
    // 1 = success. 0 = insufficient stock.
    // One SQL. No read-modify-write. No lock.
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.StockRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class AtomicStockService {

    private final StockRepository stockRepo;

    public AtomicStockService(StockRepository stockRepo) {
        this.stockRepo = stockRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public boolean buyStock(String symbol, int quantity) {
        int updated = stockRepo.decrementStock(symbol, quantity);
        return updated > 0;
    }
}
```

```text
SQL: UPDATE stock_holdings
     SET available_quantity = available_quantity - 1
     WHERE symbol = 'ICICIIPL' AND available_quantity >= 1

If quantity was 10: 1 row updated. available_quantity = 9.
If quantity was 0:  0 rows updated. Insufficient.
No read-modify-write race. No version conflict. No lock.
```

### How do you decide which strategy to use?

The decision framework is based on contention level, tolerance for retries, and whether the operation can be expressed as an atomic SQL statement.

| Contention | Strategy | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Low (rare conflicts) | Optimistic | No lock overhead, rare cheap retry |
| Medium (occasional) | Optimistic + bounded retry | Retry handles occasional clashes |
| High (frequent) | Pessimistic | Avoids retry storms, serialized |
| Very high (hot key) | Atomic conditional UPDATE | One SQL, no read-modify-write |
| Extreme (millions/sec) | Shard + queue | Spread contention across sub-rows |

```java
// Decision code pattern:
//
// Step 1: Default to optimistic locking (@Version on entity)
// Step 2: Monitor retry rate per entity/account in production
// Step 3: If retry rate > threshold for a specific row:
//         Escalate to pessimistic or atomic conditional UPDATE
// Step 4: For extreme hot keys:
//         Shard the quantity across N sub-rows
//         Or route orders through a serializing queue
```

### What about sharding for extreme contention?

For extremely hot rows where even pessimistic locking becomes a bottleneck, shard the quantity across multiple sub-rows to spread contention.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.StockShardRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

@Service
public class ShardedStockService {

    private static final int SHARD_COUNT = 10;
    private final StockShardRepository shardRepo;

    public ShardedStockService(StockShardRepository shardRepo) {
        this.shardRepo = shardRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public boolean buyStock(String symbol, int quantity) {
        int shard = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(SHARD_COUNT);
        int updated = shardRepo.decrementShard(symbol, shard, quantity);
        if (updated > 0) return true;

        for (int i = 0; i < SHARD_COUNT; i++) {
            if (i == shard) continue;
            updated = shardRepo.decrementShard(symbol, i, quantity);
            if (updated > 0) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Stock sharding:
  stock_shards table:
  symbol='ICICIIPL', shard=0, quantity=1
  symbol='ICICIIPL', shard=1, quantity=1
  ...
  symbol='ICICIIPL', shard=9, quantity=1
  Total: 10 units across 10 shards.
  5,000 requests spread across 10 shards.
  Contention per row reduced by 10x.
```

### What should a senior engineer know about contention analysis?

Senior engineers should measure contention in production rather than guessing. Monitor the optimistic retry rate per entity and per specific row. If a particular row consistently triggers retries, that is the signal to escalate from optimistic to pessimistic or atomic conditional UPDATE.

```java
// Monitoring approach for National Bank of India:
//
// 1. Counter: jpa.optimistic.retries (tagged by entity and ID)
//    Increment on every ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException
//
// 2. Timer: jpa.pessimistic.lockwait (tagged by entity)
//    Measure time spent waiting for pessimistic locks
//
// 3. Alert: if retry rate for a specific account > 10/min
//    Escalate that account to pessimistic locking
//
// 4. Dashboard: top-10 most contended rows
//    Helps identify candidates for sharding or queue routing
```

### What are common mistakes in locking strategy selection?

The most common mistakes involve using the wrong strategy for the contention level.

```java
// WRONG: pessimistic locking on a cold row
@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
Optional<Account> findByAccountId(String id);
// Rajesh's savings: rarely accessed concurrently.
// Pessimistic locking wastes concurrency for no benefit.

// CORRECT: optimistic locking for cold rows
// Just use @Version on the entity. No @Lock needed.

// WRONG: optimistic locking on a known hot row
@Version private Long version;
// Corporate pooled account with 50 concurrent tellers:
// Constant retry storms. Throughput tanks.

// CORRECT: pessimistic or atomic conditional for hot rows
@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
Optional<Account> findForUpdateByAccountId(String id);
```
